      "proposal": {
        "hypothesis": "The best measured T3 variant (1f43154a52dc4fb5, 0.10414 s) used a scaled half-vector, while the measured combined cache variant was slightly slower and skipping powf was slower still. This candidate tests a different reuse: for normalized L and Vneg, N·(L−Vneg) = N·L−N·Vneg. ShadeTileC already computes N·L, so lazily compute N·Vneg once per pixel after the first front-facing light and replace the normalized-half-vector dot product with this scalar identity times the same reciprocal half-vector length. It preserves the BRDF algebraically and leaves powf, light colors, attenuation, culling, and traversal unchanged; floating-point ordering still needs controller validation, and no speedup is assumed.",
